From 3829892488cbb6929a3d2a925a463fee8bfbc762 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grafici patch export <patch-export@localhost>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:04:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Clip projected geometry before signed pixel conversion

PixelVector now stores int16_t coordinates, matching GFX. Project in the
selected computation type, reject non-finite points and nonpositive display
dimensions, clip lines and filled triangles against the display rectangle,
then narrow only finite coordinates in range. Line intersections handle
opposite finite extremes without overflow in their coordinate difference.

Window projection preserves coordinates outside [0,1]; removing per-vertex
clamping avoids false edge lines and distorted triangles. Clipping follows
projection and each subdivision leaf, preserving polar/custom transforms.
The physical display is the clipping boundary; subwindows are transforms,
not additional stencil masks. Tiny filled markers obey the same boundary.

Clipped triangles use two fixed seven-point buffers (112 bytes of coordinate
payload for FP32, plus locals/call stack). No heap or framebuffer is added.
Default clear_screen() uses native GFX fill; explicit windows retain projected
clears. This prevents a basic line sketch from retaining triangle clipping.

Validation: geometric intersections, off-screen rejection, NaN/infinity,
finite extremes, zero/maximum display sizes, rotated/polar windows and 2,000
guarded polygon cases pass under UBSan and float-cast-overflow. The actual
GFXcanvas16 integration confirms full triangle coverage, exact horizontal
rasterization and native clear. The FP16 Uno R3 example builds; final linked sizes are recorded in the
series README.

Regression sources and the configurable host runner live in Grafici-Test:
https://github.com/cattanimarco/Grafici-Test/blob/f922bc61317a4e84718346f90e9042bf436b04b5/tests/README.md
No test sources are bundled in this library patch.
